;; The remaining distance to an appliance OS, as a dependency graph.
;;
;; The ORDER IS NOT WRITTEN HERE. Each node states only what it needs, and
;; os/aiueos/scripts/maturity-plan.cljk computes a reverse topological order --
;; dependencies first, dependents after -- and refuses on a cycle. That split is
;; deliberate: a hand-written sequence is an assertion nobody can check, and it
;; goes stale the moment one edge changes. An order derived from edges cannot
;; disagree with the edges.
;;
;; :evidence is how we know a node is open, and it names a measurement or a
;; contract rather than an opinion. A node with no evidence is a belief.
;;
;; :owner-gated marks work that cannot be done by an agent at all -- a
;; credential, a hardware decision, a purchase. These are sorted like any other
;; node so their position is honest, but the loop must not spin on them.
{:format :aiueos.maturity-plan/v1
 :benchmark :chromeos
 :measured-baseline {:union {:core 75 :of 115 :pct 65.2}
                     :release-image {:core 66 :of 115 :pct 57.4}
                     :build {:core 63 :of 115 :pct 54.8}
                     :at "2026-09-09" :by "os/aiueos/scripts/os-coverage.cljk"
                     :note "The release image reads HIGHER than the build. The
                             earlier 25.4 measured it built without a define its
                             own gate requires."}

 :nodes
 {:release-image-boots-to-completion
  {:title "The release image boot reaches the end instead of stopping partway"
   :needs []
   ;; Two halves, and neither is stalled for want of effort.
   ;;
   ;; The hang does not reproduce: nine runs, zero hangs, and the detector
   ;; written for it never fired once. Chasing a bug that will not appear is
   ;; how a loop spends a day producing nothing, so this waits for a
   ;; recurrence rather than for another guess. The instrumentation to catch
   ;; it is already in place, which is what makes waiting reasonable rather
   ;; than lazy -- the next occurrence will name itself.
   ;;
   ;; The other half is understood and is not an OS defect: the suite requires
   ;; a fabricated input event that a shipping image must not contain, so it
   ;; cannot pass a production image by construction. That is a gate design
   ;; decision with a real trade-off -- whether a BOOT TRANSPORT gate should
   ;; assert input at all -- and it is the owner's to make, not an agent's.
   :blocked-on :no-reproduction-and-a-gate-design-question
   :evidence "Restated 2026-09-09 after measuring, and the restatement is the
              point. This was filed as an enumeration difference because the
              GPT boot emitted no VIRTIO_GPU_OK or VIRTIO_INPUT_OK while PCI_OK
              appeared in both. Making pci_result observable showed something
              simpler: in that run the GPT boot emitted no PCI line AT ALL,
              neither PCI_OK nor PCI_FAIL, and ended at APIC_TIMER. It is not
              enumerating differently, it is stopping earlier. The stopping
              point also moves -- GUEST_INPUT on one run, APIC_TIMER on two
              others. The ESP boot of the same sources reaches 76 markers with
              PCI_RESULT input-rng-base=111 and VIRTIO_INPUT_OK, so the code
              path itself is sound.
              SHARPENED the same day, and the earlier restatement was half
              wrong. Every 0x6f exit now names its own line, and the release
              image stops at main.c:3058 -- the branch taken when
              `pci_result & 4` is clear, the virtio-input bit. The bits read
              input-rng-base=011 on the GPT image against 111 on the ESP
              build, through the SAME suite with the same devices and the same
              QMP input injection, so it is not an artifact of a hand-built
              command. So this IS an enumeration difference after all; the run
              that showed no PCI line at all is a second, earlier failure mode
              that remains uncharacterised.
              CORRECTED AGAIN, and this is the third reading of this node in
              three iterations. Counting the devices the scan SAW settles it:
              both profiles report input-devices-seen=1. The device is present
              in the release image's boot exactly as in the build's. What
              differs is that virtio_input() FAILS TO BRING IT UP -- seen=1,
              ok=0 -- so this is neither an enumeration difference nor an early
              stop. It is device initialisation.
              Each earlier reading came from a coarser signal: absent markers,
              then a missing PCI line, then a result bit. Each was consistent
              with what was visible and wrong about what was happening. The
              measurement that settled it was the cheapest of the three.
              Next: what inside virtio_input() fails. A plausible line, not yet
              measured, is memory layout -- the GPT image is far larger than
              the ESP directory, so firmware allocation differs and the DMA
              pages the probe needs may land differently.
              SETTLED, and the answer retires the alarm this node was built
              on. virtio_input fails at its LAST exit, the one after
              #ifdef AIUEOS_INPUT_SMOKE_SYNTHETIC -- no event arrived and no
              synthetic one was compiled in. smoke-qemu-uefi.sh builds ITS
              kernel with AIUEOS_INPUT_SMOKE_SYNTHETIC=1;
              build-release-image.sh does not. So the gate was booting an image
              built without a test-only define through a suite that requires
              the event that define fabricates.
              Built with it, the release image reports input-rng-base=111,
              input-fail-line=0 and 79 markers -- MORE than the ESP build's 76
              -- and measures 57.4 percent rather than 25.4.
              The release image was never half broken. What is real, and is
              the honest residue of this node, is that the suite cannot pass a
              genuine production image by construction: a shipping image must
              not fabricate input events, and the suite requires one. That is a
              gate design problem, not an OS defect, and
              smoke-qemu-release-image.sh already shows the intended shape by
              setting the define before it builds.
              REMAINING, and it is the original observation with the noise
              removed: the release image does not stop in the same place twice.
              virtio_input's exit now names itself
              (no-event-no-synthetic-build, one of four reasons rather than a
              line number that moves), and with that in place the USB gate
              began failing on transport DIVERGENCE instead -- one run with the
              disk side short, the next with the usb side short. The gate's
              central claim is that the two transports produce identical
              evidence, and it compares two single samples of a boot whose
              stopping point moves. It was passing because both samples used to
              stop in the same place, not because the boot is deterministic.
              So the node's real content is the nondeterministic stop, and the
              gate needs to reach the same point twice before its equivalence
              assertion means anything.
              LOCALISED 2026-09-09. The early stop is a HANG, not a fault:
              exit 124, no exception in the whole interrupt log, only timer and
              MSI-X. The link map now emitted beside the kernel resolves the
              spinning address: the BSP sits in virtio_blk_sector_io at
              0x1163c2, taking vector 0x23 -- 35, which the evidence names as
              the virtio-blk MSI-X vector -- over and over. The completion
              interrupt ARRIVES, repeatedly, and the wait loop never finishes.
              So the long-running nondeterministic stop is a storage-path hang,
              not input and not enumeration.
              Two wrong readings were discarded on the way, both from the same
              mistake. The last events in the log sit in task_loop, which is the
              idle AP, not the stuck BSP -- the two stack pointers differing by
              exactly 0x1000 were two CPUs. And -smp 1 does not isolate the BSP:
              this guest requires cpu>=2 and fails ACPI honestly. Filtering
              events by address, against the map, was what separated them."
   :why-first "Nothing else can be proved on the shipping artifact while it
               stops partway. Largest measured gap: 76 markers on the build
               against about 30 on the image."}

  :reproducible-build
  {:title "Two builds of one tree produce one image"
   :needs []
   :done true
   :evidence "CLOSED 2026-09-09, and the node was founded on my own error.
              It said three builds of an unchanged tree gave three KERNEL.ELF
              hashes because build identity is embedded. The tree was not
              unchanged -- I had been editing C sources between those builds --
              and the identity header holds version, commit[:12] and a dirty
              flag, with no timestamp in it, so it could not have caused this.
              Measured properly: two builds back to back with nothing touched
              give a byte-identical KERNEL.ELF (6eb33f67...), and building into
              a different output directory gives the same bytes again. So the
              build is reproducible on this machine and path-independent.
              NOT claimed: reproducibility across machines, toolchain versions
              or clocks. Those were never measured and the node does not assert
              them."
   :why-first "Was: two images cannot be compared by hash. They can."}

  :amd-ivrs-isolation
  {:title "DMA isolation on AMD (IVRS), not only VT-d"
   :needs []
   ;; The discovery half is done and landed. The rest -- IVHD parsing and an
   ;; AMD-Vi device table -- cannot be exercised here: QEMU has the amd-iommu
   ;; device and does not present the guest an IVRS table, measured with and
   ;; without intremap=on. Writing a parser against a table no run can produce
   ;; is writing it blind, so this waits for the board rather than being
   ;; guessed at. Marked so --next moves on instead of returning here every
   ;; twenty minutes.
   :hardware-gated true
   :evidence "kernel/main.c gates the RTL8125 path behind
              AIUEOS_PHYSICAL_NETWORK_QUALIFICATION and its own comment calls it
              test-only because AMD-IVRS isolation is not implemented."
   :checked "2026-09-09: suspected this was the wrong requirement for the board
             and it was not. The K16 is an AMD Ryzen 7 7735HS, so IVRS is
             exactly right and Intel VT-d is the part that does not apply here.
             Recorded so the next reader does not re-run the check."
   :consequence "The isolation row's proven markers are Intel VT-d, measured
                 under QEMU emulating Intel. On this board that coverage
                 transfers to nothing -- the one row where a QEMU number is
                 actively misleading about the hardware."
   :progress "2026-09-09: NOT hardware-gated, which was the open question.
              QEMU 10.0.3 provides `amd-iommu`, so the board's IOMMU class can
              be emulated, and AIUEOS_TEST_IVRS=1 now boots the guest behind
              AMD-Vi. First run of aiueos on that platform: it boots, the suite
              exits 0, ACPI is fine, and DMA policy reports
              `dmar=absent test-only-unisolated`. So the guest fails SAFE on the
              real platform -- it declines to claim isolation it does not have
              -- and the remaining work is an IVRS parser and AMD-Vi device
              table, which is substantial C and testable without the board.
              The mode asserts nothing yet on purpose: there is no AMD path to
              assert, and a permanently red gate is worse than none."
   :correction "Later the same day, and this walks back the sentence above.
                NOT hardware-gated was too strong. QEMU has the amd-iommu
                DEVICE and does not give the guest an IVRS TABLE: with the
                device attached, with and without intremap=on, the guest
                reports AIUEOS_IVRS_ABSENT no-amd-iommu-described. So the AMD
                path cannot be exercised here after all, and this node is
                gated on either a QEMU configuration that presents IVRS or the
                board itself.
                What the step did produce: acpi.c now discovers and validates
                IVRS with the same fail-closed shape as DMAR, and the guest
                reports three distinguishable states rather than one. seen
                versus present matters because collapsing them makes -this
                machine has no IOMMU- and -this machine has one we could not
                read- look identical, and the first is permission to run
                unisolated. The parser is correct by construction and untested
                against a real table, and is written down that way."}

  :tpm-key-binding
  {:title "Keys bound to a hardware root of trust"
   :needs []
   :evidence "contracts/os-coverage-v1.edn crypto row: TLS 1.3 records exist
              in-kernel, no TPM binding. Named there as the clearest single gap
              against ChromeOS.
              VERIFIED 2026-09-09 rather than quoted: zero files in the tree
              mention tpm, tcg2 or measured boot. The claim is true and this is
              from scratch.
              ADVANCED: acpi.c now discovers and validates the TPM2 table with
              the same fail-closed shape as DMAR and IVRS -- a table that claims
              to describe a root of trust and does not validate stops the boot,
              because a skipped one leaves present at 0, and no TPM is what a
              policy reads as permission to proceed without one. The guest
              reports TPM2_OK, TPM2_FAIL or TPM2_ABSENT.
              swtpm 0.10.2 is installed, so the emulator half exists now."
   :blocked-on :no-tpm2-table-reaches-the-guest
   :blocked-detail "With swtpm running and -device tpm-tis attached, QEMU and
                    swtpm report no error and the guest still reports
                    TPM2_ABSENT. The firmware never mentions a TPM. So an
                    emulated TPM does not become visible to this guest with the
                    OVMF build available here, and writing a TPM 2.0 command
                    layer against a root of trust nothing presents is the same
                    blind work IVRS was.
                    The cause is NOT established. The obvious test -- looking
                    for TPM strings in the firmware volume -- is invalid, and
                    its own control says so: DMAR also returns zero hits while
                    demonstrably reaching the guest, because the volume is
                    compressed. Lifting this needs a TPM-enabled OVMF, or the
                    board."}

